    {
      "id": "benchmark-generator",
      "name": "Benchmark generator",
      "parent": "tooling",
      "category": "infrastructure",
      "runsIn": "node",
      "purpose": "Writes specs/benchmark/spec.jsonc, a deterministic synthetic specification (396 components and 507 relationships by default) for measuring layout and rendering.",
      "unknowns": ["It writes the benchmark specification directly with node:fs, not through the workspace service or the transaction system."],
      "implementation": {
        "status": "manual",
        "modules": [
          "tools/make-benchmark.js"
        ],
        "language": "js"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "markdown-importer",
      "name": "Markdown importer",
      "parent": "tooling",
      "category": "infrastructure",
      "runsIn": "node",
      "purpose": "A command line tool that converts a Markdown specification, structured Markdown kept in another repository, into the specification file of a linked project, a specification that declares its own entity kinds and verification methods.",
      "responsibilities": [
        "Read the Markdown (white paper, roadmap, the specification documents and the verification document) and the git commit of that repository, plus the project's import map (specs/<project>/import-map.jsonc), which supplies what Exegra requires and the Markdown does not state (the project's name and title, where those documents are, and a category per component); fail with a message naming a component that has no entry in the map",
        "Work one way and deterministically: the same input gives the same bytes, it never writes to the Markdown repository and it states nothing the Markdown does not; the Markdown stays the authority and the generated file says it must not be edited by hand",
        "Record what it recognised but could not map: each kind of loss with a count and examples is printed and also written into the generated specification's system notes",
        "With --check, compare the generated text with the file on disk and exit 1 when it is out of date; usage errors exit 2"
      ],
      "unknowns": [
        "It does not analyze, validate or evaluate what it writes (it imports only the jsonc-parser library); that the output analyzes without errors is checked by the tests, not by the tool.",
        "It writes the project's specification file directly with node:fs, not through the workspace service or the transaction system, and an edit made to that file through Exegra is overwritten by the next import.",
        "It reads no default source: the source directory is given with --source or as \"source\" in the import map, and the project with --project or with --map and --out."
      ],
      "implementation": {
        "status": "manual",
        "modules": [
          "tools/import-markdown.js"
        ],
        "notes": "Not part of the running application or the agent command line, except that the markdown command renders documents with its functions; it is run by hand when the Markdown specification changes.",
        "language": "js"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "screenshot-tool",
      "name": "Screenshot tool",
      "parent": "tooling",
      "category": "infrastructure",
      "runsIn": "node",
      "purpose": "A development aid that loads the running application in Chrome, optionally runs a snippet, saves a screenshot and prints console errors.",
      "implementation": {
        "status": "manual",
        "modules": [
          "tools/shot.mjs",
          "tools/walk-shots.mjs"
        ],
        "language": "js"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "test-suites",
      "name": "Test suites",
      "parent": "tooling",
      "category": "infrastructure",
      "runsIn": "node",
      "purpose": "Core tests (node:test) over the semantic core, generators, workspace and agent command line, and Playwright browser tests of the running application: against scratch copies of this specification and of the multiplayer test fixture, and, when a project is linked locally, read-only against it (local-*.spec.js files, which are not tracked); the older browser tests run at desktop size and tests/browser/mobile.spec.js runs at phone and tablet size by touch. The browser tests run on two Playwright workers by default (EXEGRA_TEST_WORKERS overrides); each worker slot has its own pair of scratch projects (zz-test-exegra-wN, zz-test-multiplayer-wN), so edits, saves and change notices never cross workers.",
      "unknowns": [
        "Tests are evidence only through `exegra verify`; recorded results carry the records stamp of the tree and read as stale on any later edit, until verify runs again.",
        "No test measures performance, so no claim about frame rate or load time has evidence."
      ],
      "implementation": {
